      <h1>Reliable Data Pipelines Need More Than Successful Executions</h1>
      <p class="deck">Why reproducible validation matters when analytical models become more complex.</p>
      <p class="byline">By Federica Iengo <span aria-hidden="true">·</span> <time datetime={published}>15 November 2026</time></p>
    </header>

    <div class="article-body">
      <p>A data pipeline can complete successfully, produce every expected table and still leave important questions unanswered.</p>
      <p>Execution confirms that the process ran. Reliability depends on whether the resulting data preserves the relationships, definitions and business logic the analysis requires.</p>
      <p>This distinction becomes particularly relevant when several transformations contribute to the same reporting environment.</p>

      <h2>The complexity behind a final number</h2>
      <p>In e-commerce analytics, an order, its individual items and the associated payments describe different parts of a transaction.</p>
      <p>Each has its own level of detail. Bringing them together requires more than technically valid SQL: the relationships between those records must remain consistent with the metrics being calculated.</p>
      <p>A financial total, for example, needs to reflect the correct transaction grain. An aggregation can be syntactically valid while producing a misleading figure if those relationships are not handled appropriately.</p>
      <p>These issues are not always obvious in the finished report.</p>
      <p>They become easier to investigate when validation is part of the analytical workflow, rather than a final check performed after the results have been produced.</p>

      <h2>Extending validation beyond the original environment</h2>
      <p>My E-Commerce Analytics project was developed using Snowflake and dbt, with transformations supporting financial reconciliation, operational analysis and reporting.</p>
      <p>To extend its reproducibility, I built a separate validation layer using DuckDB.</p>
      <p>It exercises 15 connected SQL models from the original 28-model project, covering staging transformations, order-level and item-level facts, a calendar dimension and three monthly reporting marts.</p>
      <p>The validation uses controlled synthetic datasets and checks structural consistency alongside selected business rules.</p>
      <p>In the latest documented CI run, all 11 Python validation scripts completed successfully.</p>
      <p>This provides a repeatable way to assess the tested transformation chain independently of the original warehouse environment, while preserving the distinction between the two execution contexts.</p>

      <h2>The value of clearly defined evidence</h2>
      <p>Validation results are meaningful only in relation to what was actually tested.</p>
      <p>The DuckDB suite covers a connected subset of the original SQL models. It does not reproduce the complete Snowflake/dbt environment, execute the full 28-model pipeline or establish production performance.</p>
      <p>Those boundaries do not diminish the value of the tests. They make the results interpretable.</p>
      <p>A documented validation process should make it possible to distinguish between confirmed behavior, assumptions and areas that still require investigation.</p>
      <p>That clarity becomes especially important when analytical outputs are used to support financial or operational decisions.</p>

      <h2>Reliability as an ongoing responsibility</h2>
      <p>Analytical systems evolve. Models change, new data arrives and reporting requirements become more complex.</p>
      <p>Maintaining confidence in their outputs requires checks that can be repeated and understood beyond the moment they were first written.</p>
      <p>The additional DuckDB validation layer strengthens the reproducibility of the E-Commerce Analytics project by providing evidence for a defined portion of its transformation logic.</p>
      <p>It complements the original Snowflake/dbt work without claiming to replace it.</p>
      <p>Reliable analytics depends on more than a successful execution. It depends on being able to substantiate the results that execution produces.</p>
    </div>

